      "id": 1,
      "text": "Kief sprang to life in 1908 when the Soo Line Railroad built a station there, sparking the town's early growth.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kief%2C_North_Dakota",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Black Sea German settlers named the town after Kiev, the capital of the former Russian Empire, reflecting their heritage.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kief%2C_North_Dakota",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "A post office opened in 1909, and Kief was officially incorporated as a village in 1916, cementing its community status.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kief%2C_North_Dakota",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "In 1967, state law changed and Kief transitioned from village to city, aligning with North Dakota's new incorporation framework.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        11
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kief%2C_North_Dakota",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Liberty Baptist Church, a historic landmark, earned a spot on the National Register of Historic Places, preserving Kief's architectural heritage.",
